+# Prospective public danger alert response
+
+`--alert-policy public-danger-alert-v1` enables a separate tool-only `ww2-public-alert-response-v1` stream. Existing reaction scores and manual response policies keep their definitions and results. Historical recordings cannot acquire this prospective capture.
+
+Every off-screen native alert of kind `base`, `attack`, `air`, `unitLost` or `pointLost` requires a response. These are exactly the red danger kinds in `client/alerts.js`. Classification uses the creation descriptor alone and is identical at every difficulty. `ready` means production or building completion, and `pointWon` means a friendly objective gain. Both are informational. Scripted `event` messages have no source-defined danger classification, so they remain unknown and block certification. Every raw alert, including its count and merge identity, remains recorded.
+
+The clock starts when native public perception creates the alert. The delivered snapshot tick remains separate. A response can finish with a paid causal Space key, axis pan or minimap camera click that brings the original alert location on screen. The tool captures actual queue and action objects through private WeakMaps, with enqueue, motor start and completion ticks. It verifies the native completion descriptor against the physical input log. The camera must move, match the actual completed target and pass the shared public `onScreen` helper for the original linked alert. Space retargeting to another alert does not answer the original unless that original location also becomes visible.
+
+Native hands also log failed attempts before applying the camera. The adapter checks the live minimap/alert action and the source early-return conditions before treating its changed camera pose as an application. It does not add a gameplay flag. The pan adapter also verifies its actual WASD key, key preparation and positive hold duration, with the completed target fixed at motor start. Continuous motion may expose a marker before arrival, but the response clock stops only at the native completion. A stop-after-input request does not erase a useful completed held pan: native hands apply its camera, emit its physical input and then clear the job. A canceled waiting pan has no completion and remains censored. Completed partial pans remain censored when the original marker stays off screen. Group recipes have no response adapter in this version. A causal unsupported endpoint before the first verified camera response leaves certification unknown. Useful non-camera actions require a separate source-proven purpose adapter; ordinary command acceptance supplies no such proof.
+
+Creation and completion geometry retain only detached public cells used by the shared helper: the 3 by 3 neighborhoods at the alert and the camera focus, including levels and terrain characters. Flat maps explicitly record the absence of height. Missing terrain coverage stays unknown. Authoritative game state never enters this capture or the planner.
+
+Every required unanswered alert remains end-censored. Eventless seats retain explicit coverage; absence of native public perception cannot prove eventless coverage. Pool all event-level completed and censored durations across every seat in each mode and difficulty. Use Kaplan-Meier, with completions preceding censors at tied times. An unidentified median or unknown seat coverage cannot pass. The original bounds remain Easy 3 to 6 seconds, Normal 1.5 to 3 seconds, and Hard 0.8 to 1.6 seconds.
+
+Scores are computed from the live native stream inside its collection worker before serialization. A private sealed copy binds the stream and native input/event timelines. Altered, fabricated or deserialized streams cannot produce a native score. No generic verifier callback can override this requirement. JSON reports retain already computed prospective results and source checkpoints, rather than rescoring old logs.
+
+The focused fixture authors public hurt-shot cues and advances commander ticks manually without stepping the simulation. Native perception creates the off-screen alerts, and native hands execute real Space, pan and minimap recipes. It checks successful axis pan arrival, partial pan, useful completed held pan with a stop request, canceled waiting pan, interrupted partial pan, pan capture isolation, retargeting, interruption, wrong cause, failed camera application, zero-time alteration, incomplete geometry, eventless coverage, unknown coverage and censor ties. An independent run of 181 actual default `think` calls, with the simulation tick set manually and no simulation steps, compares full native input/command/event timelines, commander and game state, RNG, and existing private oracle measurements with capture enabled and disabled.
